    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "Diagnostic criteria for a paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome are a cancer diagnosis and the presence of onconeural antibodies (Graus et al., 2004). SPS is most commonly seen with breast and lung cancers; however, two-thirds of patients with SPS present with occult tumors (Byrne et al., 2012; Dalmau & Rosenfeld, 2013). Antiamphiphysin antibodies are SPS-specific and are the diagnostic discriminator for this paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome (Byrne et al., 2012; Graus et al., 2004).",
      "quote": "Diagnostic criteria for a paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome are a cancer diagnosis and the presence of onconeural antibodies (Graus et al., 2004). SPS is most commonly seen with breast and lung cancers; however, two-thirds of patients with SPS present with occult tumors (Byrne et al., 2012; Dalmau & Rosenfeld, 2013). Antiamphiphysin antibodies are SPS-specific and are the diagnostic discriminator for this paraneoplastic neurologic syndrome (Byrne et al., 2012; Graus et al., 2004).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_clinical",
      "value": "used",
      "quote": "The clinical criteria for all SPS syndromes are: “(1) muscular rigidity in the limbs and trunk, (2) continuous co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, (3) episodic spasms precipitated by noise, tactile stimuli, or emotional upset, (4) absence of any other neurologic disease, and (5) positive antibodies for anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)65 or amphiphysin” (Dalakas, 2009, p. 103).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
